<b>יח.</b> They come in alms - but after Parshat Hachatat he will not say yes, according to the fact that no alms come; Unless he has committed an offense for which he must commit a sin. and guilty. And guilt is hanging, sustainable Len Daina comes in charity. And the latter wrote in the name of Rashel Dish to say even after you have sinned and are guilty, let it be willed, only that he say in the language of doubt, that is: "Let it be your will, if I have committed an offense for which a sin is owed, that it be considered as if I had sacrificed a sin"; And if not, it will be as a Torah reader. And Kahai Guona will also say the same after Parshat Ashem. But the shield Avraham made it even more difficult, how can he say "If we made a commitment, you have sinned"? Ha kaima len in the matter of the sin of debai knowing in the beginning that he sinned! That is why the defender Avraham brought up, in the matter of sin, he should not say at all, "Let there be a will"; Only in the case of a certain guilty party and a contingent guilty party, he will be able to say, "Let it be, this is a condition." But if his heart was pierced lest he committed a sin and he had knowledge that he had sinned and forgot, he could also say, "I wish I had committed another sin in the way of a condition [gates of answer]":
